{"entities":[{"pmid":"2907585","text":"catalepsy","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D002375","start":32,"end":41},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"aryl-piperazine","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"-1","start":51,"end":66},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"buspirone","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D002065","start":112,"end":121},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"catalepsy","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D002375","start":132,"end":141},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"haloperidol","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D006220","start":153,"end":164},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"aryl-piperazine","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"-1","start":178,"end":193},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"buspirone","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D002065","start":207,"end":216},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"5-hydroxytryptaminergic agonists","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D058825","start":227,"end":259},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"haloperidol","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D006220","start":301,"end":312},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"catalepsy","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D002375","start":321,"end":330},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"5-hydroxytryptamine","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D012701","start":369,"end":388},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"catalepsy","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D002375","start":422,"end":431},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"5-HT","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D012701","start":463,"end":467},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"5-HT","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D012701","start":549,"end":553},{"pmid":"2907585","text":"catalepsy","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D002375","start":610,"end":619}],"gold_relations":"haloperidol | CID | catalepsy","metrics":{"precision":0.3333333333333333,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.5,"tp":1,"fp":2,"fn":0,"rel_precision":0.3333333333333333,"rel_recall":1.0,"rel_f1":0.5,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":2,"rel_fn":0},"pmid":"2907585","relations":["D002065 | CID | D002375","D006220 | CID | D002375","D002065 | CID | D006220"],"text":"Reversal of neuroleptic-induced catalepsy by novel aryl-piperazine anxiolytic drugs. The novel anxiolytic drug, buspirone, reverses catalepsy induced by haloperidol. A series of aryl-piperazine analogues of buspirone and other 5-hydroxytryptaminergic agonists were tested for their ability to reverse haloperidol induced catalepsy. Those drugs with strong affinity for 5-hydroxytryptamine1a receptors were able to reverse catalepsy. Drugs with affinity for other 5-HT receptors or weak affinity were ineffective. However, inhibition of postsynaptic 5-HT receptors neither inhibited nor potentiated reversal of catalepsy and leaves open the question as to the site or mechanism for this effect."}